1
00:00:00,327 --> 00:00:04,084
A car travels at two metres per second and accelerates smoothly.

2
00:00:10,525 --> 00:00:13,233
After four seconds it already does fourteen.

3
00:00:14,323 --> 00:00:15,709
How many metres did it cover?

4
00:00:16,328 --> 00:00:19,161
By the end of the lesson you will find the answer two ways.

5
00:00:20,329 --> 00:00:25,835
If speed never changes, motion is uniform: v equals s over t.

6
00:00:30,312 --> 00:00:33,780
Here speed grows — but grows by the same amount each second.

7
00:00:34,326 --> 00:00:40,200
Every second adds three metres per second — that is what an acceleration of three means.

8
00:00:40,525 --> 00:00:43,257
Such motion is called uniformly accelerated.

9
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:51,182
Acceleration measures how fast velocity grows: a equals the change in velocity divided by time.

10
00:00:51,427 --> 00:00:53,933
Its unit is the metre per second squared.

11
00:00:56,328 --> 00:01:01,218
We point the x axis along the motion: everything forward takes the plus sign.

12
00:01:10,295 --> 00:01:14,403
Velocity at any moment is the starting velocity plus the gain.

13
00:01:14,895 --> 00:01:19,443
The gain is acceleration times time: v equals v naught plus a t.

14
00:01:22,287 --> 00:01:27,052
Plug in our numbers: two plus three times four — fourteen metres per second.

15
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:43,252
Check the units: metres per second squared times seconds gives metres per second.

16
00:01:43,693 --> 00:01:44,498
It all fits.

17
00:01:50,288 --> 00:01:53,981
Displacement is how far the body shifted from the start.

18
00:01:54,196 --> 00:01:57,953
While direction stays the same, it equals the distance travelled.

19
00:02:02,296 --> 00:02:07,040
The formula: s equals v naught t plus a t squared over two.

20
00:02:07,294 --> 00:02:12,826
The first term is what we would cover without accelerating; the second is the boost from acceleration.

21
00:02:16,295 --> 00:02:18,428
Let's compute: two times four is eight,

22
00:02:19,581 --> 00:02:22,609
plus three times sixteen over two — twenty-four.

23
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:24,662
Thirty-two metres in total.

24
00:02:40,292 --> 00:02:44,829
Let's plot velocity against time: v equals two plus three t.

25
00:02:45,396 --> 00:02:48,180
It is a straight line: two at zero,

26
00:02:49,383 --> 00:02:51,319
fourteen after four seconds.

27
00:02:54,294 --> 00:02:58,996
The slope of the line is the acceleration: the steeper, the larger the a.

28
00:03:08,295 --> 00:03:13,008
Remember: the slope of the v–t graph equals the acceleration.

29
00:03:20,296 --> 00:03:20,936
Now the check.

30
00:03:21,594 --> 00:03:25,654
The area under the velocity graph numerically equals the displacement.

31
00:03:25,895 --> 00:03:35,022
We have a trapezoid with bases two and fourteen and height four: half the sum of the bases times the height — eight times four — thirty-two metres.

32
00:03:36,296 --> 00:03:37,532
It matches the formula.

33
00:03:37,993 --> 00:03:39,202
Two ways, one answer.

34
00:03:50,296 --> 00:03:50,769
Now you.

35
00:03:51,293 --> 00:03:55,622
A car starts from rest with an acceleration of two metres per second squared.

36
00:03:55,888 --> 00:03:57,558
How far does it go in five seconds?

37
00:04:00,289 --> 00:04:01,812
Pause the video and solve it.

38
00:04:12,294 --> 00:04:18,035
Answer: zero times five plus two times twenty-five over two — twenty-five metres.

39
00:04:20,293 --> 00:04:20,997
Two traps.

40
00:04:21,388 --> 00:04:28,229
First: plugging the final speed into the uniform-motion formula: fourteen times four is fifty-six.

41
00:04:28,496 --> 00:04:31,588
Wrong: the speed was growing, you must take the average.

42
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:36,789
Second: mixing up slope and area.

43
00:04:37,289 --> 00:04:40,752
Slope is acceleration; area is displacement.

44
00:04:43,293 --> 00:04:53,889
To sum up: v equals v naught plus a t; s equals v naught t plus a t squared over two; the area under the v–t graph is the displacement.

45
00:04:54,094 --> 00:04:58,256
Graph-area problems appear in UNT mocks — now you will recognise them.

